            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to deceive, then the Latin for to call to witness."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: doceō, docēre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "doceō, docēre — \"to teach\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Magister puerōs litterās docet. The teacher teaches the boys their letters."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "doceō, docēre, docuī; its participle follows in a later lesson. Two accusatives, the person and the thing: puerōs litterās docet. English doctor, first a teacher, and docile."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "doceō is inherited from Proto-Italic dokeō (EtymDB), usually taken as a causative beside decet \"it is fitting\": to make someone take a thing in. English doctor and doctrine come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
