              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "praecipiō, praecipere — \"to instruct, to advise; to take beforehand\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Medicus mihi praecēpit ut quiēscerem. The doctor told me to rest."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "praecipiō, praecipere, praecēpī; prae + capiō: to take beforehand, then to lay down a rule: praecipiō tibi ut, I instruct you to. English precept."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "praecipiō is built from prae \"before\" and capiō \"to take\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): to take in advance, then to tell in advance. English precept comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
